## The convention

**Cut forward. Never move a tag. Attach a release note.**

When a released version turns out to be bad, the fix is a **new** version that
reverts or corrects the problem — not deleting, retagging, or force-pushing over
the bad one.

- **Cut forward.** The remedy is the next version number, not a rewind. A
  consumer already on the bad build moves forward to the fix like any other
  update; nothing asks them to detect and undo an in-between state.
- **Never move a tag.** A tag that once pointed at the bad release keeps
  pointing at it, forever. Retagging makes "what did version X actually
  contain" stop having one answer — for anyone who already built against it,
  cached it, or filed a bug against it.
- **Attach a release note.** The new version's release note says plainly what
  was wrong in the previous one and what this version changes about it. A
  silent re-release ships the same confusion back out with a new number on it.

This is the whole convention. Everything below is where a project makes it
concrete enough to actually follow at 2am.

